# No AI traces in pushed content

Anything that reaches the remote (commits, branches, PRs, tags, release notes, and project docs meant for the team) must read as **human-authored project work** with **no AI attribution or tooling fingerprints**.

## Never push

- `Co-authored-by:` / `Signed-off-by:` / `Reviewed-by:` trailers naming AI tools, bots, or agents
- Tool or product names in commit messages or PR text: Cursor, cursorAgent, Composer, Copilot, ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, etc.
- Comments or docs that say content was AI-generated, assisted, or produced by an agent
- Placeholder markers: `Generated by…`, `AI-assisted`, `TODO(AI)`, boilerplate agent sign-offs
- Debug or session artifacts: chat transcripts, prompt dumps, `.cursor/` paths referenced in user-facing docs unless intentional project config

## Commits and PRs

- Messages describe **what changed and why** — neutral, technical, concise
- No meta-commentary about how the change was produced
- Review the full diff before push; scrub accidental AI wording in comments or docs touched in the same change

```text
# Bad
Fix workflow

Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>

# Good
Fix GHCR publish workflow frontend build in CI.
```

## Code and docs

- Do not add comments whose only purpose is to explain that AI wrote the code
- Match existing project tone; avoid generic AI filler in README/MD updates
- `.cursor/rules/` is local IDE config — fine in repo; do not reference it in commit messages or team docs unless documenting editor setup

## Before every push

1. Scan commit message(s) and PR body for AI names and co-author trailers
2. If the environment auto-appends trailers (e.g. `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>`), **remove them** — amend, rebase, or rewrite — before pushing
3. Never use `--no-verify` to bypass checks unless the user explicitly asks; still manually remove AI trailers
